---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

// Caliper's product page. Cited from both App Store listings as the marketing URL, and it is where
// /caliper/privacy hangs off.
//
// WHAT THIS PAGE MUST NOT DO: claim a tier, a repeatability figure, or a number of rigs. Those are
// owned elsewhere (caliper/docs/RIGS.md, the measurement pipeline docs) and an unmarked restatement
// here is how every contradiction in this repo started. It describes what the app IS.
//
// PRICE: free, no paid tier, both routes, iOS included. Owner: caliper/docs/PRICING.md, 2026-09-18.
const updated = '19 September 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Caliper · Warren Labs"
  description="A headphone measurement app that tells you whether to believe the result. Free, for Mac and iPhone."
>
  <Nav variant="home" />

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Caliper</span>
      <h2>A measurement you can believe</h2>

      <p class="sec-lede">
        Caliper measures a headphone on an acoustic fixture and tells you how much of what you are
        looking at is real. Free, for Mac and iPhone.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b><a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/caliper-headphone-measurement/id6813610070">Download
        Caliper on the App Store</a></b> — free, one purchase covers Mac and iPhone.
      </p>

      <h3>The problem it solves</h3>
      <p>
        Any app can draw a curve. The hard part is knowing whether the same headphone, measured
        again, would give you the same curve — because if it would not, the differences you are
        reading belong to how it was sitting on the fixture, not to the headphone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most published headphone measurements are a single curve with no statement of uncertainty.
        Caliper asks for a few captures instead of one and spends them working out its own error
        budget, so every verdict it gives you is measured against a floor it actually established.
      </p>

      <h3>How it works</h3>
      <p>
        <b>The duplicate pair.</b> Two sweeps of one ear with nothing touched in between. Any
        difference between them is the instrument and the room, not the headphone — which makes it
        the smallest difference the run is entitled to call real. A run will not complete without
        one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Seatings.</b> Take the headphone off, put it back on, measure again. The spread between
        seatings is the honest uncertainty of the measurement, and it is usually larger than people
        expect. How many you need is derived from how much yours actually disagree, not fixed by a
        rule — an easy headphone may need one, a difficult one eight.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>A tier, computed and never asserted.</b> What a run earns depends on what it holds. The
        app says what is missing and what to do next, and a capture taken in any order counts for
        whatever it satisfies.
      </p>

      <h3>What it will not do</h3>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <b>It will not smooth a measurement unless you ask.</b> Smoothing is a display choice and
          the stored data stays raw.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>It will not let you measure half-calibrated.</b> One capsule corrected and the other
          not makes every left-versus-right difference an artefact of the calibration rather than
          the headphone, so it refuses.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>It will not judge distortion it cannot see.</b> At low drive the harmonics sit in the
          noise, so it says the signal-to-noise is too low to tell rather than blaming the driver.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>It will not publish synthesised data.</b> There is a simulated fixture for trying the
          app without hardware; every capture it makes is stamped, and exporting a run that holds
          one is refused.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Price</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Free.</b> No paid tier, no subscription, no in-app purchase, on both Mac and iPhone.
      </p>

      <h3>Privacy</h3>
      <p>
        No account, no analytics, no telemetry, and nothing uploaded in the background. The one
        thing Caliper ever sends is a measurement you choose to submit, and it shows you what is
        being sent first. <a href="/caliper/privacy">The privacy policy</a> is specific about it.
      </p>

      <p class="byline">Last updated {updated}.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
